  "thumbnailAlt": "Rental Application form beside a photo of a hand holding house keys in a bright living room",
  "content": {
    "intro": "A rental application form is a form that collects a prospective tenant's identity, rental history, employment, income, references and consent to screening so a landlord or property manager can choose a reliable tenant. This template follows the order most landlords review in — current home, then job and income, then references — and lets applicants upload pay stubs securely.",
    "audience": "Independent landlords, property managers, letting agents, student housing providers and housing co-ops.",
    "useCases": [
      "Accepting applications after a showing or open house",
      "A link in rental listings on Zillow, Craigslist or Facebook Marketplace",
      "Screening several roommates applying for one unit",
      "Renewing or transferring tenants between properties in a portfolio"
    ],
    "tips": [
      "Turn on save and resume so applicants can come back after finding a landlord reference or pay stub.",
      "Use a PDF template to create a filled application file for each applicant for your records.",
      "Set a closing time in Form Settings when the unit is taken, so applicants are not left waiting."
    ],
    "faq": [
      {
        "question": "What should a rental application form include?",
        "answer": "The applicant's full name, date of birth and contact details; current and previous addresses with landlord contacts; employer, job title and monthly income; personal references; other occupants; pets and vehicles; the desired move-in date and lease term; and signed consent to credit and background checks."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can a landlord ask for proof of income?",
        "answer": "Yes, landlords commonly ask for recent pay stubs, an offer letter or bank statements, and many look for income of around two and a half to three times the monthly rent. Some cities and states limit income rules or protect housing vouchers, so check your local law."
      },
      {
        "question": "What questions should a rental application avoid?",
        "answer": "Under the US Fair Housing Act, avoid questions about race, color, religion, sex, national origin, familial status or disability, and some states add more protected classes. Ask the same questions of every applicant and judge them on the same written criteria."
      },
      {
        "question": "Do I need the tenant's consent for a background check?",
        "answer": "In the US, landlords generally need written permission to pull a credit report, and must give notice if they reject an applicant based on it. This template includes a consent checkbox and a typed name; run the actual check through a tenant screening service."
      }
    ]
  },
